  "story": {
    "patterns": {
      "P01": {
        "id": "P01",
        "title": "Progressive layer reveal",
        "how": "Never show a full architecture at once. Build it one layer at a time, in data-flow order:",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P02": {
        "id": "P02",
        "title": "Step transport",
        "how": "Any process with an order gets play / pause / next / back / reset / speed, visible on screen as a transport bar, with a step counter (stage 4 / 9). The current state stays on screen at every step; the viewer must never have to remember the previous frame.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P03": {
        "id": "P03",
        "title": "State made visible",
        "how": "Anything internal that controls behaviour is drawn beside the thing it controls, not described:",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P04": {
        "id": "P04",
        "title": "Live parameter experiment",
        "how": "One control changes one number; the picture redraws immediately and the outcome is measured on screen.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P05": {
        "id": "P05",
        "title": "Before / after split screen",
        "how": "| Left | Right |",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P06": {
        "id": "P06",
        "title": "Data moving on edges",
        "how": "Dots, packets or marching dashed lines travel along the arrows. Used everywhere data physically moves: ETL, HTTP, Kafka events, HDFS block replication, Spark shuffle, JDBC round trips, retrieval, tool calls.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P07": {
        "id": "P07",
        "title": "Matrix / heatmap",
        "how": "Grid with per-cell colour intensity. Used for: table data, attention scores, embeddings, confusion matrix, correlation, data-quality grids, the residual stream.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P08": {
        "id": "P08",
        "title": "Code trace",
        "how": "The current source line highlights while variables, stack frames, heap objects and output update beside it. Reference arrows point at identity, not equality — two names pointing at one object share one arrow head.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P09": {
        "id": "P09",
        "title": "Terminal simulation",
        "how": "The command types character by character, then output appears, then the filesystem / process / network state visibly changes. Errors are shown and read, not skipped.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P10": {
        "id": "P10",
        "title": "Plan and lineage tree",
        "how": "Query as an operator tree: scan → filter → join → aggregate → sort → output, with row counts flowing upward and fan-out named when rows-out exceeds rows-in. Column lineage draws source column → transformation → result column, and lists separately the columns read but not returned.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P11": {
        "id": "P11",
        "title": "Architecture topology with failure injection",
        "how": "Components carry capacity, utilisation and health. Then one component is failed on purpose, and the film follows: propagation → retry → timeout → queue build-up → circuit breaker → shed → recovery.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P12": {
        "id": "P12",
        "title": "Dashboard plus cause-and-effect",
        "how": "When a metric moves — success rate, p95, lag, freshness, mismatch count, error rate — the cause is visible in the same frame, on the other side of the screen. A dashboard alone is a picture; a dashboard with its cause is a lesson.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P13": {
        "id": "P13",
        "title": "Timeline",
        "how": "A horizontal time axis with named events. Used for: transaction lifecycle, NRT freshness, SLA windows, test phases, deployment, incident reconstruction, project evolution.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P14": {
        "id": "P14",
        "title": "Decision tree",
        "how": "One question at a time, answered on screen, narrowing to a choice. Used for: picking a store family, batch vs stream, ETL vs ELT, index or not, model family.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P15": {
        "id": "P15",
        "title": "Star schema and cube",
        "how": "Fact in the centre, dimensions around it. Animate join, slice, dice, drill-down, roll-up. The cube is translucent; slicing removes a plane, dicing selects a sub-cube, drill-down subdivides a cell.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P16": {
        "id": "P16",
        "title": "Risk and dependency board",
        "how": "Kanban cards: risk · dependency · impact · owner · status · mitigation. Used in the D&IM, DR and delivery chapters.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P17": {
        "id": "P17",
        "title": "Prediction before reveal",
        "how": "Before the animation runs, the film asks the viewer one question and pauses on it for 2–3 seconds with a visible beat marker. Then it plays, then it puts expected vs actual side by side. This is the single highest-retention device in the film — use it at least once per act.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P18": {
        "id": "P18",
        "title": "Failure-first",
        "how": "The canonical arc, and the reason this curriculum works:",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P19": {
        "id": "P19",
        "title": "3D, only where depth is the point",
        "how": "Permitted: HDFS block placement and replication, cluster topology, embedding space, the curriculum atlas, CPU↔memory↔disk movement, architecture evolution.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P20": {
        "id": "P20",
        "title": "Callback and zoom-out",
        "how": "Every act ends by zooming out to the same architecture diagram and lighting up the component the viewer can now explain. The diagram is one asset reused eight times; it is the film's spine made visible.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P21": {
        "id": "P21",
        "title": "Row-count funnel",
        "how": "Beneath any multi-stage data operation, a bar per stage showing how many rows survived it. 14 → 13 → 11 → 2 is a sentence. This single device explains grain, filtering, joins and fan-out better than any prose.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      },
      "P22": {
        "id": "P22",
        "title": "Cost counter beside every move",
        "how": "Comparisons, swaps, page reads, network bytes, milliseconds, rows examined — counted on screen as the operation runs, not summarised at the end in a complexity table.",
        "teaches": [],
        "chapters": []
      }
    },
